= Six Thinking Hats =

{{:Six Thinking Hats/Simple}}

== Definition and provenance ==
'''Six Thinking Hats''' is a structured discussion and decision-facilitation method introduced by Edward de Bono in his 1985 book of the same title. Participants deliberately attend to different kinds of questions, signaled by six metaphorical colored hats. The hats are '''discussion roles''', not six anatomical brain regions, personality types, or empirically established independent cognitive faculties.<ref name="debono">de Bono, Edward. ''Six Thinking Hats''. Little, Brown, 1985; revised Back Bay paperback, 1999, ISBN 9780316178310. Publisher description: https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/edward-de-bono/six-thinking-hats/9780316178310/</ref>

== Six perspectives ==
* '''White''': available information, uncertainties, and missing evidence.
* '''Red''': reactions, feelings, and intuitions, acknowledged without presenting them as established facts.
* '''Black''': risks, weaknesses, and reasons for caution.
* '''Yellow''': possible benefits, value, and plausible opportunities.
* '''Green''': alternatives, creative possibilities, and modifications.
* '''Blue''': process design, facilitation, sequencing, and synthesis.<ref name="debono"/>

== Use and limitations ==
A facilitator may ask a group to explore a proposal under one perspective at a time. The sequence is chosen for the task, rather than treated as a universally required algorithm. This can make contributions easier to classify, but classification alone does not establish that decisions become more accurate, fair, or efficient. Studies in particular educational settings report positive results, yet their designs and contexts limit generalization.<ref name="elsayed">Elsayed, A. M. A., and Abbas, R. E. S. (2021). The effectiveness of De Bono's Six Thinking Hats technique in the development of critical thinking and numerical sense in mathematics education in Oman. ''Elementary Education Online'' 20(1), 1451–1460. https://www.ilkogretim-online.org/index.php/pub/article/view/3753</ref><ref name="elbilgahy">Elbilgahy, A. A., and Alanazi, F. J. (2025). Effect of applying six thinking hats teaching method for development through life span course on students opinion and critical thinking skills. ''BMC Medical Education'' 25, 884. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12909-025-07362-w</ref>

== Related concepts ==
[[Decision making]] and [[Critical thinking]] address broader processes; Six Thinking Hats is one named facilitation procedure, not a general theory of cognition.

